---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import proof from '../lib/readiness.json';
import ReadyHandoff from '../components/ReadyHandoff.astro';
import { BookOpen, ArrowRight } from '@lucide/astro';
---
<Base title="Get Ready, before an idea" description="Let your coding agent verify the actual workspace, GitHub, Cloudflare, DNS and tools, then prove a real HTTPS deployment before you choose an app."><h1>Get Ready.</h1><p class="lede">Your agent runs the checks and deploys a small reference. You only handle authentication or approval that needs a person.</p><div class="actions guide-entry"><a class="button secondary" href="/ready/guide"><BookOpen size={18} aria-hidden="true"/>First time? Follow the guide <ArrowRight size={18} aria-hidden="true"/></a></div><div class="workbench"><section><h2>Start with your agent.</h2><p>Open your project in Codex and copy the setup handoff. Your agent checks the tools, accounts and domain, then proves a deployment.</p><ReadyHandoff/><details><summary>Already prepared?</summary><p>Run <code>pnpm ready</code> in the actual Composer checkout. A fork or another workspace supplies <code>--workspace</code>, <code>--account</code>, <code>--reference-worker</code>, <code>--reference-url</code> and <code>--reference-repo</code>. The doctor checks account routing as well as HTTPS source identity. It saves failed checks, continues independent checks and resumes after authentication. Provider interruptions stay with the agent for retry; they do not automatically require another human sign-in. The receipt expires and records tool versions, source identity and the selected HTTPS proof. Changes to account, repo, domain or tools require the relevant check again. Private receipts stay on your device.</p><p>The same core can run independently through the <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/blob/main/examples/get-ready.mjs">standalone Node example</a>. Its optional setup operation installs missing pinned dependencies in a reviewed Hello checkout on the supported Mac toolchain, then resumes the same checks. Reference deployment is limited to the reviewed Hello source and an existing authorised account, Worker and route. <a href="/docs#standalone-get-ready">See the supported boundary.</a></p></details><a href="/init">Explore the Init form</a></section><aside class="ink"><h2>This operator’s verified path</h2><p class="note">This evidence belongs to the App Composer launch environment. It does not make a visitor’s environment ready.</p>{proof.checks.map(c=><div class="status"><strong>{c.label}</strong><span>{c.status}</span><p class="note">{c.detail}</p></div>)}<div class="actions"><a class="button" href={proof.url}>Open the HTTPS reference</a></div><p class="note">{proof.reviewed} · <a href="/release">Source and release proof</a></p></aside></div><section class="section split"><div><h2>You don’t need a project yet.</h2><p>The Hello World reference can be retained and customised, or left behind when your real app is ready. The verified reference uses an existing domain. Your agent checks that the selected hostname belongs to the chosen account. New domain setup and temporary preview addresses remain paths to explore; their automated setup is not yet supported.</p></div><div><h3>What the agent verifies</h3><ul><li>The actual repo, clone and GitHub permissions.</li><li>The available coding-agent and deployment tools.</li><li>The selected account, domain and DNS route.</li><li>The exact deployed identity over HTTPS.</li></ul><p>Models, registrars and browser plugins beyond this supported path remain separate integrations, never assumed from a logo.</p></div></section></Base>
